# Resume Parser — Project Documentation

## Overview
This project is a Python-based resume parser that takes a PDF resume and
converts it into structured JSON data. It was built step by step, with each
part of the pipeline developed and tested as its own script before being
combined into the final parser.

## Parser Workflow
The parser follows this pipeline:

```
PDF Resume
    |
    v
Text Extraction   (extract_text.py)
    |
    v
Content Cleaning   (clean_text.py)
    |
    v
Section Identification   (section_detector.py)
    |
    v
Structured JSON   (resume_parser.py)
```

1. **Text Extraction** — Opens the PDF and pulls out all the raw text using
   pdfplumber.
2. **Content Cleaning** — Removes extra blank lines, extra spaces, and
   unwanted symbols from the raw text.
3. **Section Identification** — Scans the cleaned text line by line to find
   common resume sections (Education, Experience, Skills, etc.) based on
   their headings.
4. **Structured JSON** — Combines all of the above, plus pulls out the
   candidate's name, email, and phone number using pattern matching, and
   saves everything into a single JSON file.

## Libraries Used
- **pdfplumber** — used for PDF text extraction. This was chosen after
  comparing it against PyMuPDF and pdfminer.six (see `Library_Comparison.pdf`
  under the research documents); pdfplumber gave the cleanest, most
  accurately ordered text of the three.
- **re** (Python's built-in regular expressions module) — used for cleaning
  text and for pattern-matching email addresses and phone numbers.
- **json** (Python's built-in module) — used to save the final parsed data
  in JSON format.

## Project Folder Structure
```
resume_parser_project/
│
├── sample_resume.pdf          # Sample PDF resume used for testing
├── extract_text.py            # Task 3 — text extraction module
├── clean_text.py               # Task 4 — text cleaning module
├── section_detector.py         # Task 5 — section identification module
├── resume_parser.py            # Task 6 — combines everything into JSON
│
├── resume_01.txt               # Sample raw extracted text
├── resume_01_cleaned.txt       # Sample cleaned text
├── resume_01.json              # Final structured JSON output
│
├── Resume_Format_Research.pdf  # Task 1 — resume layout research
├── Library_Comparison.pdf      # Task 2 — PDF library comparison
└── README.md                   # This file
```

## Installation Steps
1. Make sure Python is installed on your computer.
2. Install the required library:
   ```
   python -m pip install pdfplumber
   ```
3. Place all the `.py` files and your resume PDF in the same folder.

## How to Run the Parser
1. Open Command Prompt (cmd).
2. Navigate into the project folder, for example:
   ```
   cd C:\Users\mahno\Documents\resume_parser_project
   ```
3. Make sure your resume PDF is named `sample_resume.pdf` in the folder (or
   edit the `pdf_path` variable at the bottom of `resume_parser.py` to match
   your file's name).
4. Run the parser:
   ```
   python resume_parser.py
   ```
5. This will print the extracted name, email, phone number, and which
   sections were detected, and will save the full result into
   `resume_01.json` in the same folder.

The individual pipeline steps can also be run and tested separately:
```
python extract_text.py
python clean_text.py
python section_detector.py
```

## Known Limitations
- **Two-column and modern/creative resume layouts** — since these place
  text in columns or decorative positions, extraction can mix content
  together or lose the original reading order (see `Resume_Format_Research.pdf`
  for examples).
- **Image-based resumes** — if a resume is a scanned document or a photo
  rather than real selectable text, this parser cannot read it at all,
  since there is no text to extract. This would require OCR (optical
  character recognition), which is not part of this project.
- **Table of contents / index pages** — if a PDF contains a table of
  contents or index page with short lines that happen to match a section
  name (for example, an index entry that just says "Education"), the
  section detector can mistake that for a real heading. This was observed
  when testing against a multi-resume sample file that included a table of
  contents page.
- **Section heading variations** — the section detector relies on a list of
  known heading names. A resume using an unusual or creative heading (for
  example, "My Journey" instead of "Experience") may not be recognized as
  that section.
- **Multi-resume PDFs** — the current version processes every page in a PDF
  as one document. If a single PDF file contains multiple separate resumes
  bundled together (as was the case with the sample file used for testing
  in this project), the parser will combine all of them into one result
  rather than treating them as separate resumes.
